<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sabah</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as North Borneo</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sabah is a Malaysian state in the north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borneo">Borneo</a>, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kota-kinabalu">Kota Kinabalu</a>. Long claimed by the sultanates of Brunei and Sulu, it was administered by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-north-borneo-company">British North Borneo Company</a> from 1881, became a British crown colony in 1946 and joined Malaysia in 1963. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a> still maintains a claim to its eastern part derived from Sulu.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sabah.jpg" alt="Sabah" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mysid · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Sabah.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its Malay court culture, titles and language shaped the coastal peoples of Sarawak and <b>Sabah</b>, and its brass cannon and gongs circulated widely in the region.</blockquote><a href="/empire/brunei-sultanate">Brunei Sultanate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In May 1961 the Tunku proposed a wider federation including Singapore, <b>North Borneo</b>, Sarawak and Brunei, partly to balance Singapore's Chinese majority with the peoples of Borneo and to contain left-wing politics in Singapore.</blockquote><a href="/empire/malaysia">Malaysia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The 1878 agreement under which Sultan Jamalul Alam granted <b>North Borneo</b> to Baron Gustav von Overbeck and Alfred Dent underlies the Philippine claim to Sabah, formally raised in 1962, and the claims of the sultan's heirs, which remain a source of friction between the Philippines and Malaysia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sulu-sultanate">Sulu Sultanate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Talasea obsidian reached Vanuatu, New Caledonia and Fiji, thousands of kilometres to the south-east, and also travelled west: pieces have been identified at Bukit Tengkorak in <b>Sabah</b>, on the island of Borneo.</blockquote><a href="/culture/lapita-culture">Lapita culture · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>Some federations give all units the same powers; others are asymmetric, as Malaysia is, where <b>Sabah</b> and Sarawak joined on special terms.</blockquote><a href="/government/federation">Federation · How It Works</a></li><li><blockquote>It was written in 1921, after the Jallianwala Bagh massacre and other atrocities by the British colonial rulers, and first published in the journal <b>Sabah</b>, published from Delhi.</blockquote><a href="/songs/sarfaroshi-ki-tamanna">Sarfaroshi Ki Tamanna</a></li></ul></section>
